  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Ground-cherry Fairy Bee (Perdita halictoides) is a tiny solitary bee belonging to the Andrenidae family, known for its diminutive size and specialized foraging behavior. This species is native to New England and plays an important role in pollinating ground-cherry plants and other low-growing flora.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Minute Size",
      "description": "Among the smallest bee species, typically measuring 3-4 mm in length, making it easily overlooked in the field.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Metallic Coloration",
      "description": "Displays a distinctive metallic green or bronze sheen on the head and thorax, often with a more dull abdomen.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Plant Specificity",
      "description": "Shows strong preference for ground-cherry flowers (Physalis species), which guides identification in native habitats.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Ground-cherry Fairy Bees inhabit open, sandy or well-drained areas of New England where ground-cherry plants grow, including disturbed soils, roadsides, and gardens with native vegetation.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite their tiny size, Perdita halictoides are solitary nesters that dig individual burrows in bare ground, and females provision their offspring with ground-cherry pollen, making them highly specialized pollinators for this plant genus.",
